{"action": "experiment", "at": "2026-11-07T22:39:23+00:00", "expected": "At 32 sessions s07 failures drop substantially from 100%, though they may stay above 1%. s09 failures fall well below 60%, and s03 errors fall toward or below 1%. s04 and s06 tails shrink enough to pass no-regression against naive. s02, s05 and s01 stay well within their ceilings. s08 failures stay at about 15-20% in all four cells, so its error-ceiling criteria still fail and the bundle does not qualify. If that holds, the next step is to stop with a refusal: the s08 error ceiling cannot be reached with CU and index choices alone.", "hypothesis": "At 2 CU with all eight indexes, the remaining misses fall into two groups. The first is capacity-bound: s07 fails about 1.2% at 8 sessions and about 100% at 32 sessions (p50 near 2.9 s even at 8), s09 failures rise from 26% to 60% between 8 and 32 sessions, s03 fails 2-3% at 32 only, and the s04 no-regression check fails at 32 post-restart. All of these get worse with load, which points to CPU and I/O saturation on a 6 GB cache, not missing access paths. The second is s08, whose failure fraction is about 15-20% whatever the configuration. It was the same on the PK-only default (0.12-0.16, with every other shape timing out), on naive (0.15-0.16) and on the full-index candidate (0.16-0.20). It barely moves between 8 and 32 sessions or between warm and post-restart. Successful s08 queries stay under about 80 ms at p99. That points to a fixed set of instances that always fail: hard timeouts from a LIMIT/pkey-order plan on correlated or empty (country, region) combinations that the planner does not move to I8, or outright errors. Changing exactly one thing, CU 2 to 4 with the same indexes, separates the two groups. Prediction: at 4 CU, s07, s09, s03 and s04 improve clearly (lower failure fractions and tail latencies at 32 sessions), while s08 failures stay at about 15% or more. The hypothesis is refuted in either of two ways. If s08 failures fall below about 5%, they were capacity-driven and further work is justified. If s07 and s09 do not improve materially at 4 CU, their misses are not capacity-bound.", "iteration": 3, "kind": "proposal", "model_usd": 0.243545, "prev_sha256": "3151f113eb167c0756d02bcb14c000ff5a0eaf524adcb87d56dcfd74cec96ea2", "proposal": {"cu": 4, "index_ids": ["I1", "I2", "I3", "I4", "I5", "I6", "I7", "I8"]}, "row_sha256": "2c307124d4584e09ee6ab668cecba053fc1a9da2b1a7ab7b6368933ee346a5a1", "stop_reason": "", "usage": {"cache_creation_input_tokens": 13245, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "input_tokens": 11840, "output_tokens": 6498, "request_id": "req_011CgpNPM9nCfyg5GN9r4sYo", "stop_reason": "end_turn"}, "validator": {"bundle": "4cu-I1+I2+I3+I4+I5+I6+I7+I8", "estimated_cu_hours": 10.588, "valid": true}, "why": "4 CU with the full candidate index set is the strongest bundle in the permitted space. It has the most CPU, a 12 GB cache that covers the 11 GB dataset, and every access path. It differs from the tested bundle only in CU, so any change can be attributed to compute tier. If it fails, no cheaper bundle or index subset can plausibly qualify, because subsets remove access paths and lower tiers have less capacity. That would support an honest refusal, with s08's load- and plan-independent failure fraction as the main reason. If it meets s07, s09 and s03 and s08 unexpectedly clears, later runs can prune indexes or step back down. The cost is about 10.5 CU-hours of the 110.75 remaining."}
